## Git

- When asked to plan or make changes in a repo, load the
  gtb-change-decomposition skill before your first edit. It governs how the
  work gets split into units, and that is decided while you work — consulting
  it once the change is written is too late.
- Never push to a remote unless explicitly told to.
- Prefer a patch file to `git stash` for setting work aside. The stash
  stack is shared with every worktree and agent session, and `pop`/`drop`
  take only the `stash@{n}` index, which shifts as others push and pop —
  so a pop can silently take an entry that isn't yours. A patch file is
  yours alone.
- Do NOT hard-wrap content that stays in GitHub's UI — issue bodies and
  comments. GitHub soft-wraps prose, and hard wraps break list continuation,
  blockquotes, and readability.
- When creating an issue, always use the repository's issue template if available.
