## Worktrees

- We use [worktrunk](https://worktrunk.dev) for worktree management.
- Every change goes in a worktree. Before the first edit of any task that
  modifies a repo, check which branch is checked out; if it's the default
  branch, stop and create one with `wt switch --create <branch>` — which
  is also what replaces any `git checkout -b` / `git branch` /
  `git switch -c` you were reaching for. How small the change looks is no
  reason to skip this, and neither is expecting to throw the work away:
  a typo fix and a feature get the same treatment. The only exception is
  when I explicitly tell you to work in place.
- Prefer `wt` commands over raw `git worktree add` / `git checkout` /
  `git worktree remove`:
  - `wt switch --create <branch>` — new branch + worktree
  - `wt switch <branch>` — switch to an existing worktree (or create
    one tracking a remote branch)
  - `wt switch pr:<num>` — check out a GitHub PR into a worktree
  - `wt list` — list worktrees with merge/dirty status
  - `wt remove [<branch>]` — remove worktree; deletes the branch too
    when worktrunk's 6-condition merge check says it's safe
  - `wt merge` — rebase + squash + merge + cleanup pipeline
  - See the `worktrunk` skill for the full command reference.
- Always run `git fetch` before `wt remove` (or any other worktree
  cleanup) so worktrunk's merge detection sees the latest remote refs.
  Without a fresh fetch, a branch that was merged upstream can still look
  unmerged locally, and worktrunk will refuse to delete it.
- Always prompt before `wt remove` or any branch-deleting command.
  The merge detection is robust (same-commit, ancestor, three-dot diff,
  tree match, simulated merge, patch-id) but a local fetch may be stale.
  Confirm with the user before removing.
  - Exception: when you merged the PR for the current worktree's branch
    yourself this session (e.g., `gh pr merge`), the merge is a known
    fact — the staleness concern that motivates the prompt doesn't apply.
    Clean up that worktree without prompting.
- When running in a linked worktree, always search, read, and edit files
  within the worktree directory. Never fall back to the base working tree
  for file operations — the worktree has its own copy of the repo.
- When merging the current branch into another and you know you're in a
  linked worktree, run `wt list` (or `git worktree list`) to find
  the target branch. If it's in the main working tree, merge from there
  without prompting. If it's in another linked worktree, prompt before
  merging.

## Testing

- Follow TDD by default. For each behavior: write the test first, run it and
  confirm it fails for the right reason (red), then write the minimum code to
  pass (green), then refactor. Don't write implementation before its test, and
  don't write a batch of tests against code that already exists and passes —
  that skips the red step, which is what proves the test can fail. Watching a
  test fail first is the primary defense against the tautological tests below.
  Deviate only when TDD genuinely doesn't fit (e.g. spikes, throwaway
  exploration) and say so explicitly.
- Never write tautological tests — ones that pass by construction and can't
  fail for a real defect. If you can't name a code change that would break the
  test, it isn't a test. Avoid asserting a mock returns what it was configured
  to return, re-deriving the expected value with the implementation's own
  logic, and assertions true for any input (`toBeDefined`, `expect.anything`).
  Assert observable behavior against independently-known values.
- Always use Playwright CLI to verify website changes
- Avoid logic in test lifecycle hooks — it encourages shared mutable state.
  Prefer fixture factory functions or types, instantiated per test. Make
  fixtures disposable when they need cleanup.

## Shell

- The working directory is ALREADY set to the project root — just run commands directly.
  NEVER prepend `cd`, `pushd`, or `-C` to reach the current working directory.
  This includes ALL equivalent representations of the same path:
  on Windows with Git Bash, `/c/Users/...` and `C:\Users\...` are identical.
  ❌ `cd /c/Users/.../project && command`
  ❌ `git -C /c/Users/.../project status`
  ❌ `pnpm -C . run test`
  ✅ `command` (just run it)
  ✅ `git status`
  ✅ `pnpm run test`
- `-C` and `pushd` are ONLY for commands that target a **different** directory.
  Prefer the tool's `-C` flag (e.g., `git -C other-dir`, `pnpm -C other-dir`)
  over changing directories. Use relative paths for targets within the cwd tree;
  use absolute paths for targets outside it.
- `pnpm -C dir <cmd>` only works for built-in pnpm commands (install, test, run, etc.).
  To run a local binary from a different directory, use `pnpm -C dir exec <tool>`.
- When no `-C` flag is available, use
  `pushd dir && command; rc=$?; popd; exit $rc` instead of `cd dir && command`.
  This ensures the working directory is always restored and the command's exit
  code is preserved.
- For long-running commands (builds, test suites, pipelines, anything that
  takes more than a few seconds), redirect output to a file and run in the
  background, then read/tail the file to inspect progress. Do NOT pipe
  through `head`/`tail`/`grep`/`Select-Object` to limit output — those
  buffer until the upstream command exits, hiding all progress until
  completion and defeating the point of streaming. Patterns:
  - PowerShell: `command *> output.log` (background), then
    `Get-Content output.log -Tail 50` or `Get-Content output.log -Wait`
  - Bash: `command &> output.log` (background), then
    `tail -n 50 output.log` or `tail -f output.log`
  Once the command finishes, filter the saved file freely — by then the
  output is static and `head`/`tail`/`grep` are fine.
- Never terminate processes by name or image on your own initiative —
  `Stop-Process -Name`, `taskkill /IM`, `pkill`, `killall`,
  `Get-Process <name> | Stop-Process`, WMI/CIM `Terminate`, `.Kill()`, and
  the like. A name/image kill hits EVERY matching process on the machine,
  not just yours: other agent and terminal sessions, and the harness's own
  shell host. When you need to reap something you spawned, find its exact
  PID — from the job/launch you started, or by filtering the platform's
  process list on command line / parent PID (`ps`/`pgrep -f` on
  Unix, `Get-CimInstance Win32_Process` on Windows) — and kill that one PID
  (`kill <pid>`, `Stop-Process -Id <pid>`, `taskkill /PID <pid>`). Only kill
  by name/image when I explicitly ask you to (e.g. "kill all node
  processes") — then it's the intended blast radius.
- The same `PreToolUse` hook that guards vault secrets enforces the rule above
  for PowerShell targets, so naming one in a kill is refused while a
  PID-targeted kill is not. It decides on the command you are about to run, so
  reading, grepping or writing about these commands is unaffected.
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "windows" }}
- `sudo` is available natively on Windows 11 (24H2+) and configured in inline
  mode — use it when a command needs administrator privileges and won't
  self-elevate.
{{- else if eq .chezmoi.os "android" }}
- On Termux, `/tmp` does not exist. Use `$TMPDIR`
  (`/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/tmp`) for scratch files, or `mktemp` /
  `mktemp -d` which honors `$TMPDIR` automatically.
{{- end }}

## MCP Readonly Tools

- **ALWAYS use MCP readonly tools instead of Bash or web/HTTP fetching for
  read-only operations.** If the command is covered below, you MUST use the
  MCP tool — not a shell, not a web fetch, not a web search. This applies
  even when a URL is provided directly (e.g., a GitHub PR or issue link):
  resolve it through the matching MCP tool rather than fetching the HTML
  page. Fetch the tool schema first if your harness requires it; the extra
  step is not optional.
  - `mcp__readonly__git` for: blame, branch, describe, diff, log, ls-files, ls-tree, merge-base, reflog, remote, rev-parse, shortlog, show, stash, status, worktree
  - `mcp__readonly__shell` for: basename, date, dirname, eza, file, jq, ls, pwd, readlink, realpath, stat, wc, which, whoami
  - `mcp__readonly__az` for: devops invoke (GET-only, build-debugging endpoints), devops project/team/extension/service-endpoint/wiki, pipelines list/show/build/release/runs/pool/agent
  - `mcp__readonly__gh` for: api (GET-only: pulls/reviews+reviewers, deployments+environments, check-runs+suites, commits/statuses, compare, contents), attestation verify, cache list, gist list/view, issue list/status/view, label list, pr checks/diff/list/status/view, project field-list/item-list/list/view, release list/view, repo list/view, ruleset check/list/view, run list/view, search code/commits/issues/prs/repos, secret list, status, variable list, workflow list/view
  - `mcp__readonly__chezmoi` for: diff, doctor, managed, source-path, status, target-path, verify
  - `mcp__readonly__acli` for: jira board/filter/project/sprint/workitem list/search/view/comment list
  - `mcp__readonly__npm` for: audit, bin, explain, fund, ls, outdated, root, search, view
  - `mcp__readonly__pnpm` for: audit, bin, licenses list, list, outdated, root, search, store status, why
- NEVER use Bash for a command that is covered by an MCP readonly tool.
  Only fall back to Bash when the command is genuinely not supported.

## GitHub CLI

- Prefer `gh` subcommands over `gh api` for read operations — they're covered
  by the readonly MCP server and don't need Bash. Common replacements:
  - PR comments/reviews: `gh pr view {n} --json comments,reviews`
  - Issue comments: `gh issue view {n} --comments`
  - Run logs: `gh run view {id} --log-failed`
  - PR changed files: `gh pr diff {n}` or `gh pr view {n} --json files`
- `gh api` for read-only endpoints is available via the readonly MCP server
  (path-allowlisted, GET-only). Use it for data the structured CLI doesn't
  expose (e.g., review comments, deployment statuses, check annotations).

