## Home Assistant

- Use `hass-cli` to read and control Home Assistant. Auth is already configured
  — the credentials are supplied for you — so just run it. Don't probe for env
  vars or config files, and don't try to locate the instance on the network.
- More than one instance is reachable. A bare `hass-cli` targets mine; set
  `HASS_VAULT_INSTANCE` to reach another (`HASS_VAULT_INSTANCE=parents
  hass-cli …`). Set it per command rather than exporting it — a stray export
  silently points every later call at the wrong house. In PowerShell there is
  no per-command form, and `& { $env:… = … }` does NOT contain it: that opens
  a scope, while `$env:` writes to the process. Clear it under `finally`, or
  use a child `pwsh -c`. Never guess an id: if you need one you don't know,
  `hass-vault check` with a wrong id lists the available ones, and they are
  labels rather than secrets.
- Two different failures, easily confused. `could not resolve credentials`
  means the vault lookup failed and hass-cli never ran — `hass-vault check`
  gives the reason. If it instead reports it can't find Home Assistant on the
  local network, the wrapper was bypassed and nothing supplied credentials at
  all; that happens when the binary is invoked directly rather than found on
  PATH, so run plain `hass-cli`.
- Never run `hass-vault credential` — and note that a bare `hass-vault` is that
  same command, since `credential` is the default subcommand. It prints the
  token in full for the wrapper to read, so running it persists a long-lived
  credential in the transcript. Use `hass-vault check` for whether credentials
  resolve and `hass-vault status` for whether any are cached; neither emits a
  value. Both report per instance, and `status` lists every instance it has
  cached. See Vault Secrets below.
- If it authenticates but the server returns 401, the cached credential is
  stale — the token was rotated and the cache is still serving the old one.
  `hass-vault refresh` re-resolves it; tell me rather than reaching further.
  It refreshes the instance you name, so pass `HASS_VAULT_INSTANCE` if the
  401 came from one other than mine.
- The token is not in your environment: a wrapper puts it into hass-cli's
  process alone, so the risk is printing one rather than holding one. If you
  need to inspect a credential, print its length, never its value.
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "android" }}
- `hass-vault check` may raise a fingerprint prompt, and a master-password one
  behind it. Run it once and wait for me, rather than retrying — a second run
  just raises a second dialog. `hass-vault status` answers "are credentials
  cached?" without prompting at all.
{{- end }}
{{- end }}

## Vault Secrets

- Never run a command whose output is a credential. The ones that do are the
  `credential` subcommand of `hass-vault`, the `get` subcommand of
  `bw-session-windows.ps1` and `bw-session-termux`, `bwbio`, the
  `ssh-askpass-*` helpers, and the `bw` subcommands that read vault contents
  (`get`, `list`, `export`, `unlock`, `login`). Each exists so that a wrapper
  can read the secret off stdout, so running one by hand writes a live
  credential into the transcript — which deleting the message does not
  unwrite. The remedy at that point is rotating the secret, so the only good
  outcome is not running it.
- A bare invocation counts. `hass-vault` and `bw-session-*` both default to the
  subcommand that emits the secret, so omitting it is not the safe form.
- Diagnose with the subcommands that report shape rather than value: `check`
  for whether a credential resolves, `status` for whether one is cached. If you
  need to characterize a secret, print its length, never its value.
- A `PreToolUse` hook refuses all of the above rather than leaving it to this
  instruction (`~/.claude/deny-unsafe-commands`). Treat a refusal as the answer
  rather than as an obstacle to route around — if a secret genuinely has to be
  read, ask me to read it out of band.

## .NET

- Use the OneOf library for discriminated unions in C# versions without native
